INSERT INTO `posts` (`id`,`category_id`,`author_id`,`slug`,`title`,`excerpt`,`content`,`reading_minutes`,`meta_title`,`meta_description`,`status`,`published_at`) VALUES
(1,2,1,'iso-27001-vs-soc-2-which-first','ISO 27001 vs SOC 2: Which Should Your Organisation Pursue First?','Both frameworks signal security maturity, but they differ in output, audience and effort. Here is a practical way to decide.','<p>For growing technology companies the question is rarely <em>whether</em> to invest in security assurance, but <strong>which</strong> assurance to pursue first. ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2 are the two most requested by customers, and while they overlap considerably, they are not interchangeable.</p><h2>What each one produces</h2><p>ISO/IEC 27001 results in a <strong>certificate</strong> issued by an accredited certification body, confirming that your Information Security Management System conforms to the standard. SOC 2 results in an <strong>attestation report</strong> issued by a licensed CPA firm describing your system and the auditor\'s opinion on your controls.</p><h2>Who is asking?</h2><ul><li>Customers in Europe, the Middle East and Asia often ask for ISO 27001.</li><li>North American enterprise buyers frequently request a SOC 2 Type II report.</li></ul><h2>A practical decision path</h2><ol><li>List the last ten security questionnaires or contract clauses you received.</li><li>Identify which assurance artefact would have answered most of them.</li><li>Design a unified control set so evidence can later be reused for the other framework.</li></ol><blockquote>Tip: building controls once and mapping them to both frameworks avoids duplicate effort and audit fatigue.</blockquote><p>Whichever you choose first, start with a gap assessment. It turns a vague goal into a scoped, budgeted roadmap.</p>',2,'ISO 27001 vs SOC 2: Which Should Your Organisation Pursue First?','Both frameworks signal security maturity, but they differ in output, audience and effort. Here is a practical way to decide.','published','2026-11-01 11:34:19'),
(2,3,1,'dpdpa-readiness-checklist','DPDPA Readiness: A Practical Checklist for Indian Businesses','India\'s Digital Personal Data Protection Act changes how organisations collect and use personal data. Use this checklist to prioritise your first steps.','<p>The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 establishes India\'s first comprehensive data protection law. With the DPDP Rules notified, organisations acting as <strong>Data Fiduciaries</strong> need a structured plan.</p><h2>Start with visibility</h2><ul><li>Build an inventory of personal data, purposes and systems.</li><li>Identify where children\'s data or large volumes of sensitive data are processed.</li></ul><h2>Fix the customer-facing journey</h2><ul><li>Rewrite notices in clear, itemised language.</li><li>Implement consent capture and withdrawal that is as easy as giving consent.</li></ul><h2>Strengthen safeguards</h2><ul><li>Apply reasonable security safeguards proportionate to risk.</li><li>Prepare breach-intimation procedures for the Data Protection Board and affected individuals.</li></ul><h2>Govern and evidence</h2><p>Assign accountable owners, train staff and keep records that demonstrate your decisions. If you may be designated a Significant Data Fiduciary, plan for a DPO, periodic DPIAs and independent data audits.</p><p><em>This article is general information, not legal advice.</em></p>',2,'DPDPA Readiness: A Practical Checklist for Indian Businesses','India\'s Digital Personal Data Protection Act changes how organisations collect and use personal data. Use this checklist to prioritise your first steps.','published','2026-10-23 11:34:19'),
(3,5,1,'api-authorisation-flaws','Why Broken Object-Level Authorisation Still Tops API Risk Lists','BOLA flaws are simple to exploit and easy to miss. We explain why they persist and how engineering teams can design them out.','<p>Broken Object-Level Authorisation (BOLA) occurs when an API returns or modifies an object based on an identifier supplied by the client, without checking that the caller is allowed to access <em>that specific</em> object.</p><h2>Why it persists</h2><ul><li>Authentication is often mistaken for authorisation.</li><li>Checks are implemented per endpoint rather than centrally.</li><li>Automated scanners cannot tell which user should see which record.</li></ul><h2>Designing it out</h2><ol><li>Enforce ownership checks in a shared data-access layer.</li><li>Use unpredictable identifiers as defence in depth — never as the only control.</li><li>Write automated tests that request other tenants\' objects and expect a denial.</li></ol><p>During authorised API assessments we test every object-returning endpoint with multiple user roles and tenants, because this is where the highest-impact findings usually hide.</p>',2,'Why Broken Object-Level Authorisation Still Tops API Risk Lists','BOLA flaws are simple to exploit and easy to miss. We explain why they persist and how engineering teams can design them out.','published','2026-10-14 11:34:19'),
(4,1,1,'board-reporting-cyber-risk','Reporting Cyber Risk to the Board: Five Metrics That Matter','Boards need decision-useful information, not vulnerability counts. These five metrics help security leaders tell a clearer story.','<p>Directors increasingly carry accountability for cyber risk, yet many board packs still contain raw technical statistics. Effective reporting connects security to business outcomes.</p><h2>Five metrics worth reporting</h2><ol><li><strong>Top risks vs appetite</strong> — the handful of risks outside tolerance and the plan for each.</li><li><strong>Critical exposure age</strong> — how long high-severity, internet-facing issues remain open.</li><li><strong>Identity hygiene</strong> — MFA coverage and privileged account reviews completed.</li><li><strong>Recovery readiness</strong> — date and result of the last restore and continuity exercise.</li><li><strong>Third-party posture</strong> — critical vendors assessed and outstanding issues.</li></ol><p>Pair each metric with a trend and a decision you need from the board. That turns reporting into governance.</p>',2,'Reporting Cyber Risk to the Board: Five Metrics That Matter','Boards need decision-useful information, not vulnerability counts. These five metrics help security leaders tell a clearer story.','published','2026-10-05 11:34:19');
